window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.5","title":"Bayes' Theorem","goal":"Find the chance of really being sick after a positive test (1% prevalence, 99% sensitivity, 95% specificity), by Bayes' formula and by counting 10,000 people.","question":"A screening test for a disease is 99% sensitive (it flags 99% of sick people) and 95% specific (it correctly clears 95% of healthy people). The disease affects 1% of people. A patient tests positive and asks: what is the chance I am really sick? Can Bayes' theorem answer this, and does counting 10,000 people give the same answer?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The test's numbers","text":"These are the lesson's numbers. Sensitivity is P(positive given sick); the false alarm rate is 1 minus the specificity. Write them down once and reuse them.","code":"prevalence = 0.01      # P(sick)\nsensitivity = 0.99     # P(positive | sick)\nspecificity = 0.95     # P(negative | healthy)\nfalse_alarm = 1 - specificity\n\nprint('P(sick)                =', prevalence)\nprint('P(positive | sick)     =', sensitivity)\nprint('P(positive | healthy)  =', round(false_alarm, 2))","explain":["Store how common the disease is: 1% of people are sick. This is the prior, the chance before any test.","Store the sensitivity: the chance of a positive test when sick.","Store the specificity: the chance of a negative test when healthy.","A healthy person tests positive with probability 1 minus the specificity. This is the false alarm rate.","Print the prevalence so we can read it back.","Print the sensitivity.","Print the false alarm rate, rounded to avoid decimal noise."],"meaning":"We have three facts: 1% of people are sick, 99% of the sick test positive, and 5% of the healthy test positive by mistake. Everything else is derived from these three numbers, so we state them once and reuse them.","out":"P(sick)                = 0.01\nP(positive | sick)     = 0.99\nP(positive | healthy)  = 0.05","figs":[]},{"title":"Bayes' formula","text":"P(sick | positive) = P(positive | sick) x P(sick) / P(positive), where P(positive) adds up both ways of testing positive: sick people and healthy people with a false alarm.","code":"p_pos = sensitivity * prevalence + false_alarm * (1 - prevalence)\nppv = sensitivity * prevalence / p_pos\n\nprint(f'P(positive)         = {p_pos:.4f}')\nprint(f'P(sick | positive)  = {ppv:.4f}')","explain":["Add up both ways of testing positive: sick people who are caught, plus healthy people with a false alarm. This is the total probability of a positive.","Bayes' formula: the share of all positives that are truly sick, which is P(positive | sick) x P(sick) / P(positive).","Print the overall chance of a positive test.","Print the chance of being sick given a positive."],"meaning":"About 5.94% of all people test positive, and only 0.1667 (about 16.7%) of the people who test positive are actually sick. A positive test raises the chance from 1% to 17%, but most positives are still false alarms.","out":"P(positive)         = 0.0594\nP(sick | positive)  = 0.1667","figs":[]},{"title":"Check with 10,000 people","text":"Count people instead of multiplying probabilities. Of 10,000 people, 100 are sick, and 5% of the 9,900 healthy people get a false positive. Only the positives matter for the question.","code":"people = 10_000\nsick = round(people * prevalence)\nhealthy = people - sick\n\ntrue_pos = round(sick * sensitivity)\nfalse_pos = round(healthy * false_alarm)\n\nprint('sick:', sick, ' healthy:', healthy)\nprint('true positives:', true_pos, ' false positives:', false_pos)\nprint(f'{true_pos} / {true_pos + false_pos} = {true_pos / (true_pos + false_pos):.4f}')","explain":["Imagine 10,000 people.","About 1% of them are sick.","The rest are healthy.","Of the sick people, 99% test positive (true positives).","Of the healthy people, 5% test positive (false positives).","Print how many are sick and healthy.","Print the true and false positive counts.","Among everyone who tested positive, print the share who are sick."],"meaning":"Of 10,000 people, 100 are sick and 9,900 are healthy. There are 99 true positives and 495 false positives, so 99 / 594 = 0.1667. Counting gives the same answer as the formula, and it shows why: the healthy group is so big that its 5% errors outnumber the sick.","out":"sick: 100  healthy: 9900\ntrue positives: 99  false positives: 495\n99 / 594 = 0.1667","figs":[]},{"title":"Why the prior matters","text":"Keep the same test and change only how common the disease is. The same positive result means very different things for a rare and a common disease.","code":"for prior in [0.001, 0.01, 0.10, 0.30, 0.50]:\n    pos = sensitivity * prior + false_alarm * (1 - prior)\n    print(f'prevalence {prior:>5.1%}: P(sick | positive) = {sensitivity * prior / pos:.2%}')","explain":["Try several prevalences, from very rare to very common.","Recompute the total chance of a positive for each prevalence.","Print the chance of being sick given a positive, as a percentage."],"meaning":"With the same test, a positive result means a 1.94% chance of being sick when the disease affects 0.1% of people, 16.67% at 1%, 68.75% at 10%, 89.46% at 30% and 95.19% at 50%. The meaning of a positive test depends heavily on how common the disease is.","out":"prevalence  0.1%: P(sick | positive) = 1.94%\nprevalence  1.0%: P(sick | positive) = 16.67%\nprevalence 10.0%: P(sick | positive) = 68.75%\nprevalence 30.0%: P(sick | positive) = 89.46%\nprevalence 50.0%: P(sick | positive) = 95.19%","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"prev <- 0.01; sens <- 0.99; spec <- 0.95\np_pos <- sens * prev + (1 - spec) * (1 - prev)\nsens * prev / p_pos                 # Bayes' formula\n\nsick <- 10000 * prev                 # 100\ntrue_pos  <- sick * sens             # 99\nfalse_pos <- (10000 - sick) * (1 - spec)   # 495\ntrue_pos / (true_pos + false_pos)","explain":["Store prevalence, sensitivity and specificity on one line, separated by semicolons.","Total chance of a positive test: caught sick people plus false alarms.","Bayes' formula for P(sick | positive).","Number of sick people in 10,000: 100.","True positives: sick people the test catches.","False positives: healthy people the test wrongly flags.","Share of positives that are sick, by counting."],"expect":"P(positive) = 0.0594; P(sick | positive) = 0.1667 both ways (99 of 594)","read":"Both calculations print 0.1667. The first is Bayes' formula (p_pos is 0.0594); the second is 99 true positives out of 99 + 495 = 594 positives. Same answer, two routes.","note":"Both routes give 0.1667, about 16.7%, the same as Python."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE p_pos = 0.99*0.01 + 0.05*0.99.\nCOMPUTE ppv = 0.99*0.01 / p_pos.\nCOMPUTE true_pos = 10000*0.01*0.99.\nCOMPUTE false_pos = 10000*0.99*0.05.\nCOMPUTE ppv_counts = true_pos / (true_pos + false_pos).\nFORMATS p_pos ppv ppv_counts (F8.4).\nLIST VARIABLES=p_pos ppv true_pos false_pos ppv_counts.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so we have one row to compute in.","Start the data block.","One row containing the number 1.","End the data block.","Total chance of a positive: sick and caught plus healthy and flagged.","Bayes' formula: the share of positives that are sick.","True positives per 10,000 people.","False positives per 10,000 people.","The same answer from the counts.","Show four decimals for the probabilities.","Print all results."],"read":"In the LIST output look at p_pos (0.0594), ppv and ppv_counts (both 0.1667), and true_pos and false_pos (99 and 495). The formula and the counts agree.","note":"SPSS has no Bayes menu, so we use Compute Variable on one row. Expect 0.0594, 0.1667, 99, 495 and 0.1667."},"stata":{"code":"display \"P(positive) = \" (0.99*0.01 + 0.05*0.99)\ndisplay \"Bayes: \" (0.99*0.01 / (0.99*0.01 + 0.05*0.99))\ndisplay \"true positives: \" (10000*0.01*0.99)\ndisplay \"false positives: \" (10000*0.99*0.05)\ndisplay \"counts: \" (99 / (99 + 495))","explain":["Probability of a positive test: display works like a calculator.","Bayes' formula for the chance of being sick given a positive.","Number of true positives out of 10,000 people.","Number of false positives out of 10,000 people.","The same chance from the counts: 99 out of 99 + 495."],"read":"The five lines print .0594, .1666667, 99, 495 and .1666667. Stata drops the leading zero, so .1666667 is 0.1667. The formula and the counts agree.","note":"display works as a calculator. Stata prints .0594 and .1666667 without a leading zero; these are the same as Python's 0.0594 and 0.1667."},"conclusion":"A person who tests positive has about a 16.7% chance (99 in 594) of being sick, even though the test is 99% sensitive, because the disease is rare and false alarms from the large healthy group outnumber true cases. The caution: the answer depends on the prevalence, so the same test result means more in a group where the disease is common.","practice":{"scenario":"An email filter flags 95% of spam and also wrongly flags 3% of legitimate email. A company finds that 20% of its incoming mail is spam. An employee sees a flagged message and asks how likely it is truly spam. The company also wonders what happens in a quieter inbox where only 2% is spam.","data":"P(spam) = 0.20, P(flagged | spam) = 0.95, P(flagged | legitimate) = 0.03. Also try spam shares of 0.02 and 0.50.","tasks":["Find P(flagged), the overall chance a message is flagged, when 20% is spam.","Find P(spam | flagged) for spam shares of 2%, 20% and 50%.","Check the 20% case by counting 10,000 messages."],"hint":"Add the two ways to be flagged (spam caught plus legitimate mistakes), then divide the spam-caught part by that total. Wrap the formula in a function so you can reuse it for each prior.","solution":{"code":"def posterior(prior, sens=0.95, false_alarm=0.03):\n    p_flag = sens * prior + false_alarm * (1 - prior)\n    return sens * prior / p_flag\n\nprint(f'P(flagged) when 20% is spam = {0.95 * 0.20 + 0.03 * 0.80:.4f}')\nfor prior in [0.02, 0.20, 0.50]:\n    print(f'spam share {prior:>4.0%}: P(spam | flagged) = {posterior(prior):.4f}')\n\nn = 10_000\nspam = round(n * 0.20)\nprint('check with counts:', round(spam * 0.95), 'true flags,', round((n - spam) * 0.03), 'false flags')\nprint(f'P(spam | flagged) by counting = {1900 / 2140:.4f}')","answer":"When 20% is spam, P(flagged) = 0.2140 and P(spam | flagged) = 0.8879. The result changes with the spam share: 0.3926 at 2%, 0.8879 at 20% and 0.9694 at 50%. Counting 10,000 messages gives 1,900 true flags and 240 false flags, and 1900 / 2140 = 0.8879 again. A flag is much more trustworthy when spam is common.","out":"P(flagged) when 20% is spam = 0.2140\nspam share   2%: P(spam | flagged) = 0.3926\nspam share  20%: P(spam | flagged) = 0.8879\nspam share  50%: P(spam | flagged) = 0.9694\ncheck with counts: 1900 true flags, 240 false flags\nP(spam | flagged) by counting = 0.8879","figs":[]}}});
